-- =============================================================================
-- THE CORRECTNESS STATEMENT for the λ□ -> Java backend, and the list of what a
-- proof still owes.
--
-- Nothing here is proved. That is the point: the deliverable is a STATEMENT
-- precise enough that its obligations are nameable, plus -- in
-- `Formal/CorrectInstances.ard` (examples project) -- instances discharged by
-- computation, and one REFUTATION. Read this file for what correctness means
-- here; read that one for what is known about it.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LINEAGE. This is the second attempt. The first is on branch
-- `lambox-formal-semantics` (`Correctness.ard`, `CorrectnessInstances.ard`),
-- which stated the theorem, listed THREE obligations, proved seven instances,
-- and recorded where it stalled. Three things have changed since, and two of
-- them make this cheaper:
--
--   * THE STORE IS GONE. The old `ValRel` was a relation over a `Store`,
--     because an `Rt.Data`'s fields lived in the heap; and its obligation (2),
--     `StoreExt`, was false as stated, because the `fix` knot WROTE into an
--     already-allocated array (its finding (C): "the store is not monotone").
--     `fix` is a local class now, `vData` holds values, and obligation (2) and
--     finding (C) are both simply gone -- not worked around, deleted.
--   * `compileExpr` THREADS A `Path`, not a `String`, and `renderName` is
--     proved injective (`Formal/NodePathUnique.ard`). This does not remove the
--     substitution obstacle (below), but the old file's remark that the two
--     sides are "not even syntactically comparable" is now a statement about
--     paths rather than about invented strings.
--   * THE AXIOM ORACLE EXISTS, and it makes the theorem HARDER, not easier.
--     The old file's happiest finding was that `RtSpec` "costs nothing here --
--     λ□ has no rule for an axiom, so every case where Rt's arithmetic could
--     matter is VACUOUS in the theorem". That vacuousness is gone: λ□ now
--     evaluates axioms, so the theorem depends on the two runtimes agreeing.
--     That is `OracleAgree` below. It is FALSE at `rtLong` -- proved in
--     `Formal/CorrectInstances.ard` (`oracleDisagrees`), with the arity half
--     holding and the computed VALUE disagreeing -- which is what motivated
--     the int63 representation. The pair this project now ships is `rtInt63`,
--     where the witness that refuted it agrees.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE VALUE RELATION.
--
-- Lives here rather than with the agreement checks because it is part of the
-- STATEMENT: "the generated Java computes what λ□ says" is only as strong as
-- what "computes the same thing" means. `Formal/Agreement.ard` imports it, so
-- there is one relation and not two.
--
-- Decidable and fuel-indexed (a `Bool`), so that instances can be discharged by
-- computation; `ValRel` wraps it as the proposition the theorem states.
--
-- FIRST-ORDER VALUES ONLY, and this is a real boundary rather than a shortcut:
-- a λ□ `lambda`/`fix` corresponds to a Java closure, and the two are equal only
-- extensionally ("applied to related arguments they give related results"),
-- which is not decidable and needs a step-indexed logical relation. The same
-- boundary the verified-extraction literature reports: first-order data
-- interoperates, higher-order does not. Those cases are `false`, and a
-- partially-applied axiom is in the same class (see the header of
-- `Formal/Agreement.ard`).
--
-- IT READS THE KIND OFF THE TERM, and needs no parameter at all.
--
-- This function used to be `agreesWith (norm : Int -> Int) ...`, with `agrees`
-- and `agreesLong` instantiating it for the two backend representations. That
-- was the symptom that the representation choice was in the wrong layer: a
-- correctness statement whose notion of "these two values correspond" takes a
-- COMPILER FLAG as an argument cannot be read without knowing the build
-- configuration. With the kind in the term (`LambdaBox.PrimTag`), each clause
-- knows which arithmetic it is talking about and there is one relation again.
--
-- AND THE COMPARISON IS EXACT. The payload is `Fin int63Mod`, so a λ□ value is
-- already normalized and nothing is reconciled on the way: this is plain
-- equality, not the "one bit pattern, two readings" fudge `wrap64` performed.
-- An out-of-range λ□ literal is not representable, so there is no case left for
-- a normalization to rescue.
--
-- Fuel, because the recursion goes through an array of fields.
\func agrees (n : Nat) (t : LBTerm) (v : Value) : Bool \elim n
  | 0 => false
  | suc n => \case t, v \with {
    | box, vBox => true
    -- A `Uint63` is held in a `long`'s non-negative half, so the Java value is
    -- the payload unchanged.
    | prim (primInt63, i), vLong j => intEqB (pos i) j
    -- A 64-bit value is held as its BIT PATTERN on the λ□ side and read signed
    -- by the JVM, so the reinterpretation happens here -- once, in the clause
    -- for the kind that needs it, rather than as a parameter of the whole
    -- relation. (Nothing compiles to this kind yet; see `JavaAxioms.ard`.)
    | prim (primInt64, i), vLong j => intEqB (signed64 i) j
    -- A constructor: same tag, same fields, pointwise. The fields include the
    -- inductive parameters on both sides (constructor-block form on the λ□
    -- side, every `construct` argument passed to `new Rt.Data` on the Java
    -- side), so there is no offset to account for here.
    | construct _ c args, vData tg fs => (c == tg) and agreesAll n args fs
    | _, _ => false
  }

\func agreesAll (n : Nat) (ts : Array LBTerm) (vs : Array Value) : Bool \elim ts, vs
  | nil, nil => true
  | t :: ts, v :: vs => agrees n t v and agreesAll n ts vs
  | _, _ => false

-- The relation as a proposition: "some amount of fuel sees the correspondence".
\func ValRel (t : LBTerm) (v : Value) : \Type => \Sigma (n : Nat) (agrees n t v = true)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The name under which the backend realizes an axiom.
--
-- The two oracles are keyed differently on purpose -- `LBSpec` by `Kername`,
-- `RtSpec` by the runtime's `JIdent` -- and this is the function that relates
-- them, by going the way the compiler goes: mangle, then look the mangled name
-- up in `javaAxioms`, then read off the runtime constant. `nothing` means the
-- backend compiles that axiom to a throwing stub rather than to a runtime
-- constant, and then there is nothing to agree about.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func axiomRtName (kn : Kername) : Maybe JIdent => \case axiomImpl kn \with {
  | just (jField _ nm) => just nm
  | _ => nothing
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- OBLIGATION (3), and the primary hypothesis: THE TWO RUNTIMES AGREE.
--
-- CompCert's treatment of `external_call`, which both semantics already follow
-- by taking their runtime as a parameter: the compiler is correct MODULO the
-- runtime doing the same thing on both sides, and that "modulo" is written down
-- instead of assumed.
--
-- Two halves, and the first is not optional. If the arities disagree, the two
-- sides saturate at different points -- λ□ computing while Java is still
-- collecting, or the reverse -- and the value half is never even reached.
-- Writing the arity half down is the same contract `RtSpec.builtinArity` and
-- `LBSpec.axiomArity` each state alone; this is the statement that they match.
--
-- `jargs` is indexed by `args.len`, which is how "same length, pointwise" is
-- said here (the same device `evalConstruct` uses).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func OracleAgree (spec : LBSpec) (rt : RtSpec) : \Type =>
  \Sigma
    (\Pi (kn : Kername) (nm : JIdent)
      -> axiomRtName kn = just nm
      -> axiomArity {spec} kn = builtinArity {rt} nm)
    (\Pi (kn : Kername) (nm : JIdent) (args : Array LBTerm) (jargs : Array Value args.len)
         (v : LBTerm)
      -> axiomRtName kn = just nm
      -> (\Pi (i : Fin args.len) -> ValRel (args i) (jargs i))
      -> axiomRun {spec} kn args = just v
      -> \Sigma (jv : Value) (builtinRun {rt} nm jargs = rOk jv) (ValRel v jv))

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE THEOREM.
--
-- `CorrectGeneric` is the real statement. `Correct` is NOT an instance of it --
-- it is what `CorrectGeneric` YIELDS at the shipped pair, and only given
-- `OracleAgree lbAxioms rtLong`. The distinction matters, because that
-- hypothesis is false: `Formal/CorrectInstances.ard` refutes `OracleAgree` at
-- this pair directly (`oracleDisagrees`) and refutes `Correct` at a program
-- separately (`int63RefutesCorrect`). Neither is derived from the other, so
-- neither leans on `CorrectGeneric`, which nobody has proved.
--
-- Three things about the shape are deliberate, and two are inherited from the
-- first attempt:
--   * the conclusion is `JEval`, i.e. `\Sigma (n : Nat) (runClass ... n = ...)`
--     -- "terminates" is "some clock suffices", the functional big-step idiom
--     (Owens-Myreen-Kumar-Tan).
--   * AN EXCEPTION OUTCOME IS NOT ALLOWED. `JEval` demands `rvValue`, not
--     `rvThrown`. This is the real content of the statement: it forbids the
--     `Rt.noBranch`, `Rt.unbound`, `Rt.freeVar` and ClassCastException paths for
--     any program λ□ can evaluate.
--   * the class name is the compiler's own; nothing depends on it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func progClassName : JIdent => "Prog"

\func CorrectGeneric : \Type =>
  \Pi (spec : LBSpec) (rt : RtSpec) -> OracleAgree spec rt ->
    \Pi {p : LBProgram} {v : LBTerm} -> EvalProgram spec p v ->
      \Sigma (jv : Value)
             (JEval rt (compileClass progClassName p) bodyMethodName jv)
             (ValRel v jv)

-- The shipped pair. REFUTED -- see `Formal/CorrectInstances.ard`.
\func Correct : \Type =>
  \Pi {p : LBProgram} {v : LBTerm} -> EvalProgram lbAxioms p v ->
    \Sigma (jv : Value)
           (JEval rtInt63 (compileClass progClassName p) bodyMethodName jv)
           (ValRel v jv)

-- `Correct` AT ONE PROGRAM, with the clock named rather than existential.
--
-- This is the form the refutation attacks, and the shape is chosen carefully,
-- because the two obvious variants both prove less than they look like they do:
--
--   * `Correct` itself quantifies the clock EXISTENTIALLY (via `JEval`), so
--     contradicting it means contradicting every clock at once -- which needs
--     "a bigger clock computes the same value", i.e. obligation (1), which is
--     open. A counterexample run at one clock is not enough.
--   * a clock-fixed version quantified over ALL PROGRAMS,
--     `\Pi n -> \Pi {p} ... runClass ... n = ...`, is refutable for a reason
--     that has nothing to do with correctness: some program needs more fuel
--     than `n` (`ExamplePeano.addProgram` already needs 2000). Refuting THAT
--     would be a statement about fuel, dressed up as a statement about the
--     backend.
--
-- Fixing the program and the clock together avoids both. The program below is
-- then chosen so that the clock demonstrably SUFFICES -- the run finishes and
-- returns a value -- which leaves the value relation as the only thing that can
-- fail, and so makes the refutation a statement about the translation.
\func CorrectAtProgram (n : Nat) (p : LBProgram) (v : LBTerm) : \Type =>
  EvalProgram lbAxioms p v ->
    \Sigma (jv : Value)
           (runClass rtInt63 (compileClass progClassName p) bodyMethodName n = rOk (rvValue jv))
           (ValRel v jv)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE REMAINING OBLIGATIONS, and where each now stands:
--
--   (1) `FuelMono` is PROVED: `Formal/FuelMono.ard` (`fuelMono`, plus the
--       any-amount and whole-program forms `evalExprMonoPlus`,
--       `runClassMonoPlus`, `jevalAtLeast`).
--   (2) `SubstLemma` is FALSE AS STATED: `Formal/SubstRefuted.ard`
--       (`substLemmaFalse`). Nothing relates `a'` to `av`; see that file for
--       why even the obvious repair is not enough, and what is.
--   (2') ITS REPAIR IS DONE, as a lemma on TERMS rather than on generated
--       Java: `Formal/CSubstLam.ard`'s `substCommute`, used through a
--       closure-relating value relation (`VR`/`EnvRel`, Formal/CorrectLam.ard).
--
-- AND `CorrectGeneric` ITSELF IS FALSE (`Formal/CorrectRefuted.ard`), at the
-- trivial oracle pair, for two reasons that are not about the backend: a
-- program whose result is a function has no `ValRel`-related Java value
-- (`higherOrderRefutes`), and an OPEN program's substitution captures, which no
-- scope-respecting compiler can match (`captureRefutes`). The repaired form
-- restricts to CLOSED programs and relates closures; it is PROVED on the
-- λ-fragment (`box`/`bvar`/`lambda`/`app`), for every oracle pair, in
-- `Formal/CorrectLam.ard` (`lamProgramCorrect`, and `lamProgramCorrectFO`
-- for a first-order result). Remaining: `letIn`, `construct`/`case`/`proj`,
-- `fix`, `const`, `prim`, and the oracle.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (1) FUEL MONOTONICITY. A successful run must survive a bigger clock.
--
-- Needed in every case of the induction, because `Eval` is `\elim t` (keyed on
-- the term) while `evalExpr` is `\elim n` (keyed on the clock): a proof does
-- induction on the derivation while EXISTENTIALLY producing a clock, and then
-- has to combine the clocks of sub-derivations. It is also what would lift the
-- refutation from `CorrectAt` to `Correct`.
--
-- Mechanical and without design risk, as predicted: one lemma per function of
-- the mutual cycle (`newObject` does not recurse and needs none). It came out
-- at under 250 lines rather than the size of `Formal/JavaEval.ard`, because
-- every clause is `bindMono` of the induction hypotheses.
\func FuelMono : \Type =>
  \Pi {rt : RtSpec} {cls : JClass} {n : Nat} {env : Env} {e : JExpr} {r : ExprOut}
   -> evalExpr rt cls n env e = rOk r
   -> evalExpr rt cls (suc n) env e = rOk r

-- (2) THE SUBSTITUTION LEMMA -- the crux, and unchanged in substance since the
-- first attempt.
--
-- λ□'s `Eval` is SUBSTITUTION-based: `evalBeta` evaluates `csubst av 0 b`. The
-- generated Java is ENVIRONMENT-based: a closure captures `cenv`, and `bvar n`
-- compiles to a lookup. So induction on `Eval` produces a goal about a
-- substituted TERM while the Java side has an extended ENVIRONMENT, and nothing
-- bridges them.
--
-- It cannot be stated as an equality of generated code. `compileExpr` threads a
-- `Path` used to invent binder names, so `compileExpr cenv pth (csubst av 0 b)`
-- and `compileExpr (a' :: cenv) pth' b` are different Java -- different
-- variable names, different paths -- and only their BEHAVIOUR agrees. Hence the
-- statement below quantifies over `evalExpr` on both rather than relating the
-- two `JExpr`s.
--
-- The cost is the mutual dependency this creates: stated semantically, the
-- lemma needs the logical relation, and the logical relation needs the lemma.
-- That is why the first attempt put this at weeks rather than days, and why it
-- is last.
--
-- AND AS WRITTEN IT IS FALSE (`Formal/SubstRefuted.ard`): `a'` is unconstrained,
-- so `b = bvar 0`, `av = box`, `a' = "x"` has the left side return `Rt.BOX`
-- and the right the string. Kept as the record of what was claimed.
\func SubstLemma : \Type =>
  \Pi {cenv : Array JExpr} {pth pth' : Path} {av b : LBTerm} {a' : JExpr}
      {rt : RtSpec} {cls : JClass} {n : Nat} {env : Env} {r : ExprOut}
   -> evalExpr rt cls n env (compileExpr cenv pth (csubst av 0 b)).2 = rOk r
   -> evalExpr rt cls n env (compileExpr (a' :: cenv) pth' b).2 = rOk r

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LIMITATION (L), WITHDRAWN. Recorded rather than deleted, because it drove a
-- design decision and two theorems while it stood.
--
-- IT SAID: the statement is too weak about Lean's `Nat`, because `agrees`
-- reconciles a λ□ `prim` with a Java `long` through a normalization, and Lean's
-- `Nat` is arbitrary-precision -- so a backend that truncated it to 64 bits
-- would satisfy the theorem. `natOverflowRefutesCorrect` and
-- `oracleStillDisagreesOnNat` were the two theorems that followed once
-- `norm63` stopped absorbing the difference.
--
-- WHY IT WAS WRONG: "Lean's `Nat` is arbitrary-precision" is a fact about LEAN,
-- not about the λ□ this compiler consumes. lean-to-lambdabox erases a `Nat`
-- literal to `prim ⟨.primInt, _⟩` in `.machine` mode, bounded by
-- `BitVec.intMax 63`, and PANICS rather than exceeding it; its `PrimModel
-- .primInt` is `BitVec 63`. So every integer in our input is an i63, the
-- `Nat.*` axioms denote i63 operations, and `Formal/LbAxioms.ard` -- which had
-- modelled them on unbounded naturals -- was the side that was wrong. The two
-- theorems were proofs that the SPECIFICATION disagreed with the
-- implementation.
--
-- WHAT REPLACED IT: `natRun` wraps `add` and `mul` at 2^63, `JavaAxioms.ard`
-- routes those two to `PRIM_*_INT63`, and `natOverflowNowAgrees`
-- (`Formal/CorrectInstances.ard`) is the former counterexample discharged.
-- `Nat.sub` keeps its own truncating constant, which is a difference of
-- CONVENTION and not of number kind.
--
-- NO KNOWN COUNTEREXAMPLE REMAINS. `OracleAgree` is still not provable at the
-- shipped pair, but for a bookkeeping reason rather than a wrong answer:
-- `Formal/RtLong.ard` models fewer runtime constants than `javaAxioms` emits,
-- so the arity half fails on names the model does not know. Of the two
-- substantive obligations above, `FuelMono` is proved and `SubstLemma` refuted
-- as stated and repaired on terms; see (2') for what the repair proves.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
